1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,518
Send a radio command
to NASA’s Voyager 1 and,

2
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:07,105
by November eighteenth, it’ll take
a full day just to get there.

3
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:09,799
If the spacecraft
replies right away,

4
00:00:10,043 --> 00:00:12,399
that answer takes
another day to get back.

5
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:16,428
In October, NASA listened
twice and heard nothing.

6
00:00:16,706 --> 00:00:19,713
And before engineers sent
a troubleshooting command,

7
00:00:19,829 --> 00:00:21,734
the signal came back on its own.

8
00:00:22,848 --> 00:00:26,412
On October eighteenth,
NASA’s Deep Space Network pointed

9
00:00:26,459 --> 00:00:29,025
its antennas
at Voyager’s patch of sky.

10
00:00:29,466 --> 00:00:31,114
It didn’t detect a signal.

11
00:00:31,625 --> 00:00:34,225
Twenty-four hours later,
the team tried again.

12
00:00:34,667 --> 00:00:35,537
Still nothing.

13
00:00:35,921 --> 00:00:38,359
On the twentieth,
the signal reappeared.

14
00:00:38,684 --> 00:00:41,679
Once engineers could see what
the spacecraft had been doing,

15
00:00:41,888 --> 00:00:42,991
they found a clue.

16
00:00:43,432 --> 00:00:45,847
One group of thrusters
had gone offline,

17
00:00:46,091 --> 00:00:48,006
and its backup had switched on.

18
00:00:48,447 --> 00:00:51,617
Voyager is programmed to make
that switch automatically.

19
00:00:51,942 --> 00:00:54,543
What triggered the outage
was still under investigation

20
00:00:54,601 --> 00:00:56,087
when NASA reported it.

21
00:00:56,412 --> 00:00:59,012
Here’s what makes troubleshooting
Voyager so different

22
00:00:59,047 --> 00:01:01,532
from troubleshooting
a spacecraft near Earth.

23
00:01:02,229 --> 00:01:06,095
The first day goes by while
your command is still on its way.

24
00:01:06,791 --> 00:01:10,692
At the end of that day,
the spacecraft finally hears you.

25
00:01:11,296 --> 00:01:14,338
If it responds immediately,
the second day goes

26
00:01:14,408 --> 00:01:17,171
by while the answer
is still on its way home.

27
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:20,445
That’s forty-eight hours
of travel time alone.

28
00:01:20,689 --> 00:01:23,382
And if the answer tells you
to try something different,

29
00:01:23,626 --> 00:01:26,587
the next command starts
the whole trip again.

30
00:01:27,701 --> 00:01:29,118
On November eighteenth,

31
00:01:29,292 --> 00:01:32,937
Voyager will be about sixteen
billion miles from Earth.

32
00:01:33,448 --> 00:01:35,445
That distance is one light-day.

33
00:01:35,828 --> 00:01:38,557
There’s no marker out there
for Voyager to cross.

34
00:01:39,067 --> 00:01:42,562
The milestone is what that distance
does to a conversation.

35
00:01:43,073 --> 00:01:45,244
In September,
the engineers pulled off

36
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:46,788
a different kind of repair.

37
00:01:47,171 --> 00:01:50,829
As Voyager’s power supply
dwindles, every watt matters.

38
00:01:51,154 --> 00:01:53,487
They switched off three
devices and turned

39
00:01:53,557 --> 00:01:55,844
on three lower-power
replacements.

40
00:01:56,169 --> 00:01:58,909
Those devices help keep
the spacecraft warm,

41
00:01:59,083 --> 00:02:02,346
so the team needed to make all
three swaps together to keep

42
00:02:02,404 --> 00:02:04,192
the heat distributed properly.

43
00:02:04,517 --> 00:02:06,432
The change bought
breathing room.

44
00:02:06,757 --> 00:02:09,985
Without it, NASA says it
might have had to turn off another

45
00:02:10,031 --> 00:02:12,318
science instrument
as early as the summer

46
00:02:12,388 --> 00:02:13,758
of twenty twenty-seven.

47
00:02:14,269 --> 00:02:17,102
Voyager left Earth
in nineteen seventy-seven.

48
00:02:17,381 --> 00:02:19,343
It flew past Jupiter and Saturn.

49
00:02:19,726 --> 00:02:22,930
In nineteen ninety, it looked
back and took this picture.

50
00:02:23,313 --> 00:02:25,334
That little speck is Earth.

51
00:02:25,844 --> 00:02:28,782
It had already taken hours
for light to cross the gap

52
00:02:28,851 --> 00:02:30,756
between home and the spacecraft.

53
00:02:31,197 --> 00:02:34,819
In twenty twelve, Voyager
crossed the heliopause,

54
00:02:35,028 --> 00:02:37,791
the edge of the bubble
shaped by the solar wind.

55
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:41,007
It’s been sampling
interstellar space ever since.

56
00:02:41,007 --> 00:02:43,294
Now look at the timeline
one more time.

57
00:02:43,677 --> 00:02:46,789
At hour zero,
a signal leaves Earth.

58
00:02:47,172 --> 00:02:50,492
At hour twenty-four,
Voyager hears it.

59
00:02:50,875 --> 00:02:54,091
At hour forty-eight,
Earth can hear back.

60
00:02:54,904 --> 00:02:57,772
For a spacecraft launched
in the nineteen seventies,

61
00:02:57,888 --> 00:03:00,430
that last moment
is the remarkable part.

62
00:03:00,871 --> 00:03:02,439
After two days of waiting,

63
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:05,759
there’s still something
on the other end of the line.
